hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1716","Narrated to us Hārūn ibn Isḥāq: Narrated to us Ibn Fuḍayl; and narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr: Narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Mus-hir, both of them from al-Mukhtār ibn Fulful, from Anas ibn Mālik (RA), who said:\nOne day, the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) led us in prayer. When he finished the prayer and turned to face us, he said:\n“O people, I am your imām, so do not precede me in bowing, nor in prostration, nor in standing, nor in sitting, nor in turning away. Indeed, I see you behind me—and by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if you were to see what I have seen, you would laugh little and weep much.”\n\nThey asked, “O Messenger of Allāh, what did you see?”\nHe replied, “I saw Paradise and the Fire.”\n\nThis is the narration of Hārūn. ʿAlī did not say, “nor in sitting,” and he said, “Indeed, I see you from before me and from behind me.”\n\n---\n**CHAPTER:**"
